00:01:58.910 --> 00:02:01.430
First, let's start off by
modernizing your services.

00:02:01.430 --> 00:02:02.930
There are multiple
reasons you would

00:02:02.930 --> 00:02:04.520
want to modernize your service.

00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:07.970
For instance, it gives you
a lot more agility and speed

00:02:07.970 --> 00:02:09.810
and lets you deploy faster.

00:02:09.810 --> 00:02:11.747
So in this demo,
there are two options

00:02:11.747 --> 00:02:13.580
for how you can modernize
your applications.

00:02:13.580 --> 00:02:16.140
You can either
containerize them manually

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:18.410
or you can use
CSP Migrate, which

00:02:18.410 --> 00:02:20.360
is now called Anthos
Migrate, to be

00:02:20.360 --> 00:02:23.120
able to migrate these
applications automatically

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:26.540
from VMs running on-prem
into containers running

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:27.260
in the cloud.

00:02:27.260 --> 00:02:28.760
So for the sake of
this run-through,

00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:30.860
let's select Modernizing
with Velostrata,

00:02:30.860 --> 00:02:31.940
which is Anthos Migrate.

00:02:34.580 --> 00:02:36.920
So Velostrata
automatically containerizes

00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:39.200
your VM-based workload
and will deploy them

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:42.290
to GKE running in the cloud.

00:02:42.290 --> 00:02:44.860
So just as easily as that,
you'll have all of you

00:02:44.860 --> 00:02:47.940
a VMs now move to the
cloud as containers.

00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:49.670
Now, let's look at
the second part.

00:02:49.670 --> 00:02:51.890
We also help you standardize
config management

00:02:51.890 --> 00:02:53.870
using Anthos Config Management.

00:02:53.870 --> 00:02:56.030
What Anthos Config
Management lets you do

00:02:56.030 --> 00:02:58.250
is it lets you take a
single configuration

00:02:58.250 --> 00:03:01.970
and deploy it across the board,
declaratively and uniformly,

00:03:01.970 --> 00:03:05.540
without you having to go and do
it once at a time per platform.

00:03:05.540 --> 00:03:08.270
So no matter whether you're
running only on-prem,

00:03:08.270 --> 00:03:10.610
in Google Cloud, or maybe
even with other clouds,

00:03:10.610 --> 00:03:11.777
you can do this all at once.

00:03:11.777 --> 00:03:12.777
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Right.

00:03:12.777 --> 00:03:14.030
So now it's all standardized.

00:03:14.030 --> 00:03:16.140
SRINATH PADMANABHAN: So
now it's all standardized.

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:17.990
And so for the third
part of this demo,

00:03:17.990 --> 00:03:20.060
there's something
very exciting for me,

00:03:20.060 --> 00:03:21.680
which is a service mesh.

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:23.900
A service mesh is
basically a mechanism

00:03:23.900 --> 00:03:26.860
that lets you look deep into the
traffic that's actually flowing

00:03:26.860 --> 00:03:28.610
between your applications,
whether they're

00:03:28.610 --> 00:03:30.290
on on-premise or in the cloud.

00:03:30.290 --> 00:03:31.790
In this case, what
we're going to do

00:03:31.790 --> 00:03:33.915
is we're going to use Anthos
to implement a service

00:03:33.915 --> 00:03:36.320
mesh between on-prem
world here and the two

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:37.790
cloud worlds that we have.

00:03:37.790 --> 00:03:40.340
And by doing this, we're
able to select a service

00:03:40.340 --> 00:03:42.710
and we're able to say that
let's upgrade the service.

00:03:42.710 --> 00:03:44.780
Now, when you
upgrade the service,

00:03:44.780 --> 00:03:47.630
you pick a new version that you
want to upgrade to, but instead

00:03:47.630 --> 00:03:50.330
of upgrading everything at
once, what you're going to do

00:03:50.330 --> 00:03:52.920
is you're going to select a
small percentage off your VMs

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:55.430
or workloads and
upgrade them at once.

00:03:55.430 --> 00:03:58.100
So when you do this and you
upgrade these containers

00:03:58.100 --> 00:04:01.160
at that point in time, you
will upgrade just that 20%.

00:04:01.160 --> 00:04:03.680
So that you see everything
is running fine,

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:05.520
and you don't have
anything broken.

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:08.360
And once you are confident that
this service is working well,

00:04:08.360 --> 00:04:09.920
you can upgrade everything else.

00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:12.170
So this means now you're
able to roll out applications

00:04:12.170 --> 00:04:15.110
with limited downtime to almost
0 downtime for your customers.

